WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.440
Film Courage: Let’s talk about wounds and
fears of our main character.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.990
How severe should they be?

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:09.519
If they’re too extreme, will they scare
the viewer away?

00:00:09.519 --> 00:00:15.090
Michael Hauge: Well first of all keep in the
mind that the wound is something that always

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.820
occurs before the story actually begins.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.880
It’s in the character’s backstory.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.429
Rarely in a movie will we actually see that
wound.

00:00:24.429 --> 00:00:28.630
The only reason we would see it is if there’s
a prologue that shows the wound.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:35.100
So if you take a movie like TWISTER where
we see her father swept away by a tornado

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.940
and we flash forward to where she’s a grown
up but we know that’s a wounding experience

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.760
that is affecting her now.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.989
Or it might be in a flashback.

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:47.790
HITCH is a good example of that.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:54.720
I’m not really fond of flashbacks as a rule,
especially to reveal wounds but in that movie

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.750
it works I think because it’s such a funny
flashback as well as touching and establishes

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:08.370
such an important quality for the character
of Hitch because we see when he was in college

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.630
he had his heart broken and that’s where
he acquired the belief.

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:15.570
If I’m going to fall in love with anybody
it’s going to lead to a broken heart, which

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:21.970
leads to his identity which is I’ll help
everybody else fall in love but it’s not

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:23.300
for me.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.500
So I think it’s more palatable if you will,
but I don’t think you’re going to scare

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.430
anybody away with it because oftentimes we
won't see it.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:38.270
So keep in mind you’re not going to announce
the wound in most movies.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:42.900
Sometimes we’re aware, like in SLEEPLESS
IN SEATTLE the opening shot is a man holding

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.110
his son’s hand over a grave saying “Mommy
died.”

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.750
That’s pretty direct.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.440
Oftentimes we don’t learn what that wound
is until well into the story.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.700
It’s well half way through GOODWILL HUNTING
before we find out about the abuse that he

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:03.430
suffered when he was a child or find out the
details about his father taking a belt to

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:04.630
him.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:09.250
In GRAVITY, it doesn’t open by saying “I’m
sorry about your daughter dying.”

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:16.510
It comes out gradually after the opportunity
when the space debris comes through and destroys

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.610
the ship and so on.

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:22.710
Then Ryan (Dr. Ryan Stone) is talking to the
George Clooney character and revealing what

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:27.560
it was that happened back on earth that led
to her daughter dying and why she is carrying

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:28.580
that around with her.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.070
So it’s not going to be abrupt.

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:34.980
It’s not going to be right at the top of
the story most of the time.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:42.000
And last of all, the most effective ways to
reveal the wound is through dialogue.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.460
It’s oftentimes more powerful to hear about
a wound that the character suffered than it

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.290
would be to actually watch it on the screen.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:54.080
A great example of this is the movie LA CONFIDENTIAL.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:03:00.120
By the way I just read the Curtis Hanson,
who co-wrote and directed the movie, just

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.520
passed away yesterday.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.100
So this is the day we’re filming this thing
and I’m so sad because I think it is just

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.210
one of the great American movies and great
American screenplays.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:15.630
But the wound for Bud White, the Russell Crowe
character is when he was a child he watched

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:21.350
his father beat mother to death with a tire
iron while he was chained to the bed and he

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.940
was left there with the body for two days.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.091
That would be such an unbelievably gruesome
experience to watch on the screen.

00:03:29.091 --> 00:03:33.470
It’s such a beautiful moment when he finally
reveals it because it’s right after the

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:34.470
mid-point.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:39.900
He’s just made love to Lynn (Bracken) the
Kim Basinger character.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.290
And it shows how they have gotten closer.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:45.610
They are connected more at this level of essence.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:50.770
Finally he’s opening up about exactly what
the pain is that he’s been carrying around

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:57.740
so long and what led to him kind of becoming
this brute cop who still does everything he

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.580
can to rescue women.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.570
So I don’t think it’s something to worry
about in the way you’ve said.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:10.580
I think it’s something to skillfully weave
into the story so that we’ll feel more affinity

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:15.000
to the character and it will lead to the character’s
identity.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:21.000
Film Courage: You’re saying that we don’t
have to show the wound or explain what happened

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.850
but at some point we do reveal it?

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:28.570
Or are there some stories where we know they’re
wounded but it’s never totally revealed

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:30.420
what that wound is or how it happened?

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:31.420
Michael Hauge: Yes.

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:33.570
There are movies where it’s never clearly
stated.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:34.910
TITANIC is that way.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:40.260
It’s never clear what he identity is and
what he essence is.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.730
She longs to be passionate.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:47.800
She longs to live a fulfilled, passionate,
adventuresome life, but she’s stuck with

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.730
this mother who interfere in her life and
a rich guy who is really a jerk.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:56.140
And so it never says “Well when I was a
child this happened.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:58.380
Or when I was an adolescent this happened.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.130
We can infer some things.

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:04.870
The inferences I make is probably she either
had no father or the father abandoned them

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:05.870
early on.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:10.230
And her mother is so intent that she find
a rich guy to take care of her.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:16.660
But it’s never really stated fully, so you
can go without revealing it as long as we

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:22.190
get a clear idea of the identity that has
resulted for the hero is.

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:27.840
The main thing that I think is you as the
novelist or you as the screenwriter need to

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.050
know what the wound is.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:34.080
You need to know the backstory for your hero
well enough that you can say this is what

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.650
wounded them in the past and this is why they
are the way they are in the present.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:36.740
Question for the Viewers: What other movies
came to mind while watching this clip?

